            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 21 Development of Multicellular Organisms 1217  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "OVERVIEW OF DEVELOPMENT 1218  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Conserved Mechanisms Establish the Core Tissues of Animals 1218  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Developmental Potential of Cells Becomes Progressively Restricted 1219  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell Memory Underlies Cell Decision-Making 1220  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Several Model Organisms Have Been Crucial for Understanding Development 1220  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Regulatory DNA Seems Largely Responsible for the Differences Between Animal Species 1220  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Small Numbers of Conserved Cell-Cell Signaling Pathways Coordinate Spatial Patterning 1221  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Through Combinatorial Control and Cell Memory, Simple Signals Can Generate Complex Patterns 1221  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Morphogens Are Diffusible Inductive Signals That Exert Graded Effects 1222  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Lateral Inhibition Can Generate Patterns of Different Cell Types 1223  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Asymmetric Cell Division Can Also Generate Diversity 1224  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Initial Patterns Are Established in Small Fields of Cells and Refined by Sequential Induction as the Embryo Grows 1225  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Developmental Biology Provides Insights into Disease and Tissue Maintenance 1225  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1226  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MECHANISMS OF PATTERN FORMATION 1226  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Different Animals Use Different Mechanisms to Establish Their Primary Axes of Polarization 1226  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Studies in Drosophila Have Revealed Many Genetic Control Mechanisms Underlying Development 1228  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Gene Products Deposited in the Egg Organize the Axes of the Early Drosophila Embryo 1228  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Three Groups of Genes Control Drosophila Segmentation Along the A-P Axis 1230  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Hierarchy of Gene Regulatory Interactions Subdivides the Drosophila Embryo 1231  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Egg-Polarity, Gap, and Pair-Rule Genes Create a Transient Pattern That Is Remembered by Segment-Polarity and Hox Genes 1233  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hox Genes Permanently Pattern the A-P Axis 1233  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hox Proteins Give Each Segment Its Individuality 1234  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hox Genes Are Expressed According to Their Order in the Hox Complex 1234  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Trithorax and Polycomb Group Proteins Regulate Hox Expression to Maintain a Permanent Record of Positional Information 1235  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The D-V Signaling Genes Create a Gradient of the Transcription Regulator Dorsal 1236  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Hierarchy of Inductive Interactions Subdivides the Vertebrate Embryo 1238  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Competition Between Secreted Signaling Proteins Patterns the Vertebrate Embryonic Axes 1239  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hox Genes Control the Vertebrate A-P Axis 1240  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Transcription Regulators Can Activate a Program That Defines a Cell Type or Creates an Entire Organ 1241  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Notch-mediated Lateral Inhibition Refines Cellular Spacing Patterns 1242  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell-fate Determinants Can Be Asymmetrically Inherited Evolution of Regulatory DNA Explains Many Morphological Differences 1245  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1247  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DEVELOPMENTAL TIMING 1248  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Molecular Lifetimes Play a Critical Part in Developmental Timing 1248"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
