          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 22 Stem Cells in Tissue Homeostasis"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.526,
            0.645,
            0.923,
            0.948
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "STEM CELLS AND TISSUE HOMEOSTASIS 1279  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Stem Cells Are Defined by Their Ability to Self-renew and Produce Differentiated Cells 1280  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Epithelial Lining of the Small Intestine Is Continually Renewed Through Cell Proliferation in Crypts 1281  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Epidermal Stem Cells Maintain a Self-renewing, Waterproof, Epithelial Barrier on the Body Surface 1282  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell Lineage Tracing Reveals the Location of Stem Cells and Their Progeny 1284  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Quiescent Stem Cells Are Difficult to Identify by Lineage Tracing 1285  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hematopoietic Stem Cells Can Be Identified by Transplantation 1286  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Tissues Do Not Require Stem Cells for Their Maintenance 1289  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Response to Injury, Some Differentiated Cells Can Revert to Progenitor Cells and Some Progenitor Cells Can Revert to Stem Cells 1289  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Tissues Lack Stem Cells and Are Not Renewable 1290  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1290  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CONTROL OF STEM-CELL FATE AND SELF-RENEWAL 1291  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Stem-Cell Niche Maintains Stem-Cell Self-Renewal 1291  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Size of the Niche Can Determine the Number of Stem Cells 1292  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Asymmetric Stem-Cell Division Can Maintain Stem-Cell Number 1293  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Many Symmetric Stem-Cell Divisions, Daughter Cells Choose Their Fates Independently and Stochastically 1294  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Decline in Stem-Cell Function Contributes to Tissue Aging 1294"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
